<ContentLayout title="Aurora Security" description="Security principles, threat boundaries and verification status." eyebrow="ENGINEERING / SECURITY" heading="Security is structural." lead="Security claims must be scoped to a threat model, a concrete implementation and repeatable verification. No system is described as unbreakable.">
<section class="section shell article-content"><h2>Security review framework</h2><div class="principle-grid"><article class="principle-card"><span>01</span><h3>Threat model</h3><p>Identify assets, adversaries, entry points, trust assumptions and out-of-scope threats.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span>02</span><h3>Privilege boundaries</h3><p>Document which component can access each resource and how that authority is granted or revoked.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span>03</span><h3>Isolation</h3><p>Define failure containment and the security consequences of compromised services.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span>04</span><h3>Verification</h3><p>Link tests and reports to explicit acceptance criteria; distinguish tests from independent audits.</p></article></div><h2>Current public status</h2><p class="content-copy">No current Aurora OS or Lumen security assessment has been reviewed for this website update. This page describes the publication standard, not a security certification or guarantee.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/en/development/verification/">Verification reports ↗</a></p></section>
</ContentLayout>
